## Why This Is Asked

Assesses translating tenancy constraints into IAM data-level policies for a SaaS workload. Examines data isolation with S3 prefixes and DynamoDB keys, plus a practical Lambda trust setup.

## Key Concepts

- Data isolation via S3 prefixes and DynamoDB partition keys
- IAM trust policy for Lambda
- Resource policies with conditional keys (LeadingKeys) for per-tenant access

## Code Example

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::bucket/tenant-id/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"s3:prefix": "tenant-id/"}}
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["dynamodb:GetItem","dynamodb:PutItem"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:dynamodb:REGION:ACCOUNT:table/TenantData",
      "Condition": {"ForAllValues:StringEquals": {"dynamodb:LeadingKeys": ["tenant-id"]}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to support multi-region replication with consistent tenancy checks?
- What testing strategies would you use to detect policy misconfigurations before production?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses design for fair throughput, scalability, and auditable enforcement in a shared data lake.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant quotas; token bucket; API Gateway usage plans; DynamoDB; Lambda; S3 prefixes; CloudWatch; Kinesis Firehose; testing at scale.

## Code Example
```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, burst) {
    this.rate = rate; // tokens per second
    this.burst = burst;
    this.tokens = burst;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  allow(n=1){
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.burst, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) { this.tokens -= n; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test multi-tenant fairness under bursty conditions and validate SLA guarantees?
- What changes if large tenants typically spike during business hours?

